      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dancyville%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first Dancyville United Methodist Church was founded in 1837, followed by a Baptist church in 1838 that no longer exists, and a Presbyterian church in 1853.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dancyville%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the Civil War, two companies formed in Dancyville: the Dancyville Grays and Company L of the 9th Tennessee Infantry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dancyville%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 140‑year‑old Temple Adas Israel in Dancyville is the oldest Jewish synagogue in Tennessee still holding services.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
